  "cards": [
    {"dot": "cyan", "title": "One input, two decoding paths", "items": ["The collector preserves flow affinity across buffered worker queues. Each worker owns its assembler (internal/collector/worker.go:330-375).", "Packet layers emit individual records; enabled reassembly reconstructs conversations for protocol selection and stream decoding (worker.go:134-141; tcp_connection.go:735-842).", "Decoder selection uses -include / -exclude; protocols are not inferred from port numbers alone (gopacket_decoder.go:105-115; tcp_connection.go:749-789)."]},
    {"dot": "emerald", "title": "From bytes to useful context", "items": ["Host profiles add DNS names, geolocation and TLS JA4 observations; native DPI is conditional (internal/decoder/packet/host.go:185-279).", "Conversation analysis can harvest credentials; the inspected completion path invokes SMTP, Telnet and additional harvesters (tcp_connection.go:805-808).", "Records may instead go to CSV, JSON, Elasticsearch, channels or Unix sockets: these are selected sinks, not required services (internal/netio/writer.go:45-67)."]},
    {"dot": "amber", "title": "How to read the evidence", "items": ["This is the capture-to-investigation overview at commit 4b0b2d8. Nodes group in-process Go subsystems; filesystem outputs persist separately.", "YAML rules evaluate before output filtering, so filtered records can still raise alerts (internal/collector/filtering_writer.go:60-81).", "Web UI, CLI triage and MCP expose the analysis. Capture completeness and undecoded or encrypted traffic qualify conclusions (README.md:55,63-75; docs/mcp.md:49-54).", "Distributed collection, the separate firewall and opt-in baseline monitoring are secondary paths outside this capture overview (README.md:42,59,87-89)."]}
  ]
